# std/merge's image (design/caos-expr.md, Phase 3): this directory is a flake
# (flake.nix + flake.lock + worker), so its image is the flake-builder applied
# to the directory itself. It names the builder by a LOCAL deep-deps mount
# (DEEP-DEPS/flake-builder, declared in DEPS) -- the only way it CAN name it,
# since nothing resolves a builtin by name. Resolving this entry evaluates the
# expression, which makes the flake->image step explicit rather than leaving it
# to the server to detect.
#
# The `help` is bound HERE so that every harness offering this tool describes it
# the same way. It used to be a string literal inside llm-step, which meant a
# second harness had to copy it -- and a copied description drifts.
#
# `@param theirs` is the only thing a caller supplies. `ours` is the workspace
# it is merging into, which the launch binds; a model naming it would be naming
# the conversation it is already in.
#
# `@writer` is what tells the harness this tool's result is a PROPOSAL rather
# than a value (SPEC, "CaosTools"). It used to be known by matching the tool's
# NAME in llm-step, which is why no other tool could ever propose one.
#
# THIS TEXT IS NOW THE ONLY COPY. llm-step carried a second, hand-written
# declaration of this tool and used that one instead of this -- the exact drift
# the note above warns about, and the two had already diverged on whether they
# called it a workspace or a source tree.
HELP=<<END
Three-way merge another commit into the current source tree. `theirs` is a full
commit hash already imported into CAOS (a custom harness may also supply a named
ref snapshot); the current side is the source tree as it is now. A clean merge
advances the source tree to the merged result. A conflict advances it too, with
git's inline conflict markers in the files and a reserved `.caos/conflicts` file
listing every unresolved path -- including structural conflicts (delete/modify,
mode, binary) that have NO markers. Resolve each: edit the file (use `read` with
the stage's oid as `root` to inspect its content), then delete that path's rows
from `.caos/conflicts`. Saving the resolution removes the empty ledger and its
.caos directory if empty. Bash changes are committed automatically, just like
inline edits. Then run relevant checks.
@param theirs Full imported commit hash. A ref name works only if the harness explicitly supplied a ref snapshot.
@writer
END
IMAGE=run --base:@=DEEP-DEPS/flake-builder --in:@=. --lock:@=DEEP-DEPS/flake.lock
curry --base=$IMAGE --help=$HELP
